3, leaving White with doubled a-pawns."]},{"ply":67,"name":"34.Rd7","cls":"miss","color":"w","headline":"34.Rd7 lets the win slip: 34.Nxe5 kept White winning. White's winning chances go from 80% to 52%.","points":["Black answers 34…b3, which pushes the passed pawn to b3, close to promotion.","Better was 34.Nxe5 (+3.71), which takes the pawn on e5."],"better":"34.Nxe5 wins a pawn and threatens Rd7, winning the exchange. After 34…b3 35.Rg5+ Kh8 36.Rxh5+ Kg8 37.Rg5+, White comes out two pawns up (+3.71)."},{"ply":68,"name":"34…b3","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"34…b3! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Pushes the passed pawn to b3, close to promotion.","The next best move, 34…Rxe3, would drop Black's winning chances from 48% to 10% (+5.99).","34…b3 pushes the passed pawn to b3.","White answers 35.Rg5+, winning a pawn.","After 35.Rg5+ Kh8 36.Rxh5+ Kg8 37.Rg5+ Kh8, Black is a pawn down (+0.10)."]},{"ply":96,"name":"48…Rxc3","cls":"brilliant","color":"b","headline":"48…Rxc3!! A real sacrifice: Black gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the rook on c3 where it can be taken.","White can take, but after 49.Rxc3 b1=Q+ 50.Kh2 Qb5 the engine rates it +0.05.","Takes the knight on c3.","48…Rxc3 stops Rc6+.","White was threatening Rc6+, forking the king and the pawn. Rxc3 takes care of it.","White answers 49.Rxc3, winning the exchange."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"48…Rxc3!! gives up the rook on c3 to stay in the game: after it Black holds the balance (+0.05); anything else leaves Black losing (+6.99)."},{"title":"What Black gives up","text":"48…Rxc3 puts the rook on c3 where it can be taken by the rook on c5. That's a whole rook on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It takes the knight on c3."},{"title":"If White takes the rook","text":"49.Rxc3 is met by 49…b1=Q+. Then 50…Qb8 eyes the king through the c7 pawn, and 52…Qe8 attacks the rook on c6, which is undefended. Black ends up 6 points of material ahead: Black holds the balance (+0.06). This is White's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 48…Re1+, is clearly weaker (+6.99 against +0.05). White answers 49.Kh2, preparing Rd1 and Rb1. That would leave Black losing."}]},{"ply":124,"name":"62…Qd6","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"62…Qd6 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 31% to 2%.","points":["White answers 63.Rf7+: the rook joins the attack: it now hits 2 squares around the Black king.","Better was 62…Qd4 (+2.17): activates the queen (from 18 to 24 usable squares)."],"better":"62…Qd4 prepares …Qg1 and …c3 and activates the queen. After 63.Kh3 Qg1 64.Rg4 c3 65.Rc4 Kg6 66.Rcxc3 Kg5, Black is a pawn down (+2.17)."},{"ply":142,"name":"71…Qd6","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"71…Qd6 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 34% to 2%.","points":["White answers 72.Rb7+: the rook joins the attack: it now hits 2 squares around the Black king.","Better was 71…Qd5 (+1.84)."],"better":"71…Qd5 prepares …Qf5 and …Kh6. Engine line: 72.Re1 Qf5 73.Re7+ Kh6 74.Ree3 Qf4 (+1.84)."},{"ply":145,"name":"73.Rg5","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"73.Rg5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Shores up the rook on g3, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1).","The next best move, 73.Rf5, would drop White's winning chances from 98% to 63% (+1.39).","73.Rg5 shores up the rook on g3.","Black answers 73…c3.","After 73…c3 74.Rg4 Kh5 75.Rc4 Qe6 76.Rcxc3 Kh6, White comes out a pawn up (+11.77)."]}],"opening":{"name":"Ruy Lopez: Berlin Defense, Anti-Berlin Variation","eco":"C65","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 9.Re1: 17 moves in all, 9 by White and 8 by Black.","Black left the book with 9…Re8. In 32 games from this position, strong players played 9…Ba7 (53%) or 9…b5 (38%).","The main line goes on 9…Ba7 10.Nbd2 Ne7 11.Nf1 Ng6 12.Ng3 b5.","From here White scored 52% in those games, with 35% drawn."]}}